//? Title: Bitap: Parallel Prefix States and Substitution Windows
//? Subtitle: Exact matching, Hamming budgets, overlapping occurrences and visible machine-word states
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a byte string and a short pattern, locate every equal-length window that
matches exactly or differs in at most a chosen number of positions. Overlapping
windows and partial matches must survive while new matches begin. Bitap packs
these prefix states into machine words. We derive the update, inspect a
three-byte example, retain the original synthetic DNA motif, and exhaust a
four-letter Hamming ball. The report uses canonical match and trace APIs rather
than counting windows with a separate search implementation.
)

\toc

> The method

For pattern P of m bytes, build one mask for every possible byte c:
bit j of \(M_c\) is one exactly when \(P_j=c\). Bit j of the state R
means that the length-\(j+1\) pattern prefix matches the text suffix ending at
the current position. Reading c performs

$$
R' = ((R\ll1)\lor1)\land M_c.
$$
\alttext(Shift active prefixes one place, seed a new length-one prefix, then retain only positions whose pattern byte equals the current text byte.)

The low seed bit starts a fresh candidate at every position. Every higher bit
extends a matching shorter prefix only when its next byte agrees. Induction on
the consumed text proves the state interpretation; bit \(m-1\) signals a full
match. An earlier full match does not prevent a new overlapping one.

For at most d substitutions, keep states \(R_0,\ldots,R_k\). With every
right-hand state taken from the **previous** text position,

$$
R'_0=((R_0\ll1)\lor1)\land M_c,\qquad
R'_d=\left(((R_d\ll1)\lor1)\land M_c\right)
       \lor\left((R_{d-1}\ll1)\lor1\right),\quad d\ge1.
$$
\alttext(Level zero requires an equal byte. A higher error level can extend an equal-byte prefix at the same level or spend one substitution from the preceding level.)

The words are masked to m bits. A complete length-m window is accepted at the
smallest level whose top bit is active, which gives its actual Hamming distance.
Every window consumes exactly m text bytes. Insertions and deletions are outside
this mode. The budget is clamped into \([0,m]\): allowing more than m
substitutions cannot admit another length-m window.

> Simple example: parallel restarts in AABA

Use pattern `ABA`. The exact states after `A`, `A`, `B`, `A` are
`001`, `001`, `010`, `101`, written with the longest prefix bit on the left.
The last state has both the full prefix `ABA` and the one-byte prefix `A` active.
Thus a complete occurrence and a fresh possible occurrence coexist.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bitap_states.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <bitset>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bitap_trace("AABA","ABA",1);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(60,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=2.8;
  std::print("| byte index | byte | exact state R0 | one-substitution state R1 |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.steps.size();++i) {
    const auto& step=trace.steps[i];
    const auto exact=std::bitset<3>(step.states[0]).to_string();
    const auto fuzzy=std::bitset<3>(step.states[1]).to_string();
    diagram.node(std::to_string(i),{4.5*i,0},std::format("Read {}\nR0 {}\nR1 {}",char(step.byte),exact,fuzzy),style);
    if (i) diagram.edge(std::to_string(i-1),std::to_string(i));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",step.position,char(step.byte),exact,fuzzy);
  }
  diagram.text({6.75,-2},"Pattern ABA: bit 0 is prefix A; bit 2 is prefix ABA.");
  diagram.save_svg("build/bitap_states.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| byte index | byte | exact state R0 | one-substitution state R1 |
| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 0 | A | 001 | 001 |
| 1 | A | 001 | 011 |
| 2 | B | 010 | 011 |
| 3 | A | 101 | 101 |
\image(figures/bitap_states.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All error levels use one=active. The states come from the same core loop that finds matches; no report-side Bitap replay is used.)
\alttext(Four state boxes follow AABA. The exact states are zero zero one, zero zero one, zero one zero and one zero one. The one-substitution states are zero zero one, zero one one, zero one one and one zero one.)

> The original motif example

The original 47-byte synthetic DNA string contains `ACGTACG` twice exactly,
as well as nearby windows with substitutions. This is an invented algorithm
fixture, not an observed genome. The 41 overlapping length-seven windows are
counted directly by `bitap_matches` below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bitap_motif.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string text="TTGCAACGTACGTTAGGCAACGTCCGATCGAAGGTAAGACGTACGCC",motif="ACGTACG";
  std::vector<double> counts;
  std::vector<std::string> labels;
  std::print("| allowed substitutions | windows | start offsets | first legacy inclusive end |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (int budget=0;budget<=4;++budget) {
    const auto matches=dm::algorithms::bitap_matches(text,motif,budget);
    std::vector<std::size_t> starts;
    for (const auto& match:matches) starts.push_back(match.begin);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",budget,matches.size(),starts,
        dm::algorithms::bitap_fuzzy_search(text,motif,budget));
    counts.push_back(matches.size());labels.push_back(std::to_string(budget));
  }
  std::print("\nFirst exact start: {}; complete windows: {}.\n",
      dm::algorithms::bitap_search(text,motif),text.size()-motif.size()+1);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(counts,labels,"matching windows",dm::RGB{5,150,105})
      .title("Synthetic motif: more windows qualify")
      .x_label("allowed substitutions").y_label("overlapping windows")
      .size(850,460).save_svg("build/bitap_motif.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| allowed substitutions | windows | start offsets | first legacy inclusive end |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |
| 0 | 2 | [5, 38] | 11 |
| 1 | 3 | [5, 19, 38] | 11 |
| 2 | 5 | [5, 9, 19, 31, 38] | 11 |
| 3 | 7 | [5, 9, 19, 23, 27, 31, 38] | 11 |
| 4 | 13 | [1, 5, 9, 10, 14, 15, 19, 23, 26, 27, 31, 34, 38] | 7 |

First exact start: 5; complete windows: 41.
\image(figures/bitap_motif.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original exact and fuzzy queries are retained, with every qualifying start offset visible. Budgets give nested sets, so counts cannot decrease.)
\alttext(The matching-window counts increase from two exact matches to three, five, seven and thirteen as the substitution budget rises from zero through four.)

The legacy interfaces use different index conventions: `bitap_search` returns
a **start**, while `bitap_fuzzy_search` returns an **inclusive end**. For the
first exact seven-byte motif these are 5 and 11. Modern match records instead
use the consistent half-open interval \([5,12)\), plus the actual substitution
count. The repeated occurrences at starts 5 and 38 are separate windows.

> Checking the theory: exhaust a Hamming ball

For a fixed m-byte pattern over an alphabet of q symbols, the number of strings
at exactly d substitutions is

$$
H_d={m\choose d}(q-1)^d.
$$
\alttext(Choose the d changed positions, then choose one of q minus one different symbols at each changed position.)

For pattern `ACGT` over `ACGT`, the coefficients of \((1+3z)^4\) are
\((1,12,54,108,81)\). Their sum is 256, the number of all four-byte strings.
The program constructs those strings, asks Bitap for each full-window distance,
and compares the measured histogram with the exact formula.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bitap_hamming_ball.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string alphabet="ACGT",pattern="ACGT";
  std::vector<double> observed(5);
  const std::vector<int> predicted{1,12,54,108,81};
  for (unsigned code=0;code<256;++code) {
    unsigned digits=code;
    std::string word(4,'A');
    for (char& byte:word) { byte=alphabet[digits%4];digits/=4; }
    const auto matches=dm::algorithms::bitap_matches(word,pattern,4);
    if (matches.size()!=1) return 1;
    ++observed[matches.front().substitutions];
  }
  std::print("| exact substitutions | observed strings | binomial count | cumulative observed |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::string> labels;
  int cumulative=0;
  for (int d=0;d<=4;++d) {
    if (observed[d]!=predicted[d]) return 1;
    cumulative+=observed[d];labels.push_back(std::to_string(d));
    std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {} | {} |\n",d,observed[d],predicted[d],cumulative);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(observed,labels,"all four-byte strings",dm::RGB{37,99,235})
      .title("Exhaustive Hamming distances to ACGT")
      .x_label("actual substitutions").y_label("strings out of 256")
      .size(850,460).save_svg("build/bitap_hamming_ball.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| exact substitutions | observed strings | binomial count | cumulative observed |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 |
| 1 | 12 | 12 | 13 |
| 2 | 54 | 54 | 67 |
| 3 | 108 | 108 | 175 |
| 4 | 81 | 81 | 256 |
\image(figures/bitap_hamming_ball.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Every possible four-byte word is scored, so this is an exhaustive check rather than a random estimate. Distances are recovered from the smallest accepting error level.)
\alttext(The histogram has one word at distance zero, twelve at one, fifty-four at two, one hundred eight at three and eighty-one at four.)

If a text were independently sampled uniformly from that alphabet, the expected
number of qualifying windows would be \((n-m+1)\sum_{d=0}^k H_d/q^m\).
Linearity of expectation does not require windows to be independent; overlapping
window indicators generally are dependent. The constructed motif example is not
sampled from this model, so its counts are not a significance calculation.

> API boundaries and exact work counts

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <climits>
int main() {
  std::print("| pattern bytes | legacy exact start | legacy fuzzy end | modern exact windows |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  const std::string text(70,'x');
  for (int length:{62,63,64}) {
    const std::string pattern(length,'x');
    std::string modern;
    try { modern=std::to_string(dm::algorithms::bitap_matches(text,pattern).size()); }
    catch (const std::invalid_argument&) { modern="unsupported pattern length"; }
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",length,dm::algorithms::bitap_search(text,pattern),
        dm::algorithms::bitap_fuzzy_search(text,pattern,0),modern);
  }
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bitap_trace("abcdefgh","WXYZ",INT_MAX);
  std::print("\nRequested budget INT_MAX: effective budget {}, {} windows, {} bytes, {} word updates.\n",
      trace.effective_budget,trace.matches.size(),trace.bytes_scanned,trace.word_updates);
  std::print("Empty pattern in ab: {} boundary matches. Insertion example ABXCD versus ABCD at budget 1: {} windows.\n",
      dm::algorithms::bitap_matches("ab","").size(),dm::algorithms::bitap_matches("ABXCD","ABCD",1).size());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| pattern bytes | legacy exact start | legacy fuzzy end | modern exact windows |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 62 | 0 | 61 | 9 |
| 63 | 0 | -1 | 8 |
| 64 | -1 | -1 | unsupported pattern length |

Requested budget INT_MAX: effective budget 4, 5 windows, 8 bytes, 40 word updates.
Empty pattern in ab: 3 boundary matches. Insertion example ABXCD versus ABCD at budget 1: 0 windows.
// result_end
\caption(The historical unsupported-length sentinel is preserved. Modern helpers reject an oversized pattern explicitly, and a huge requested budget is capped before allocating or updating error levels.)

The `ABXCD` example contains a one-insertion alignment with `ABCD`, but neither
four-byte window is within one substitution. This distinguishes Hamming windows
from variable-length edit-distance matches. Empty patterns produce n+1 empty
boundary intervals in modern helpers; the two legacy first-match calls retain
their special return value zero.

For a nonempty pattern, preprocessing uses 256 masks and m assignments. A full
scan with effective budget k performs exactly \(n(k+1)\) state-word updates,
plus acceptance checks and output handling. The overall work is
\(O(n(k+1)+m+256)\), with fixed word-state storage and one result per reported
window. Tracing additionally owns \(n(k+1)\) recorded words. Work counts do not
establish a runtime advantage over another string-search algorithm.

All indices and lengths count bytes. Matching is case-sensitive, supports NUL
and arbitrary byte values, and performs no Unicode normalisation. The exact
legacy call supports patterns through 63 bytes; its fuzzy counterpart retains
the older 62-byte limit. Modern helpers support 63 bytes and use one active-bit
convention at every error level.

> Conclusion

The hand example exposes simultaneous full matches and restarts. Canonical
match records reproduce the original motif counts and index conventions, while
exhaustive enumeration verifies the Hamming-distance histogram. Native tests
also check every recorded prefix bit against direct suffix comparisons. These
synthetic and exhaustive controls establish the algorithmic claims without
introducing biological conclusions or an unrelated observed dataset.

> References

- Baeza-Yates, R. and Gonnet, G. H. (1992). *A New Approach to Text Searching*. Communications of the ACM, 35(10), 74–82. https://doi.org/10.1145/135239.135243
